\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 # Autonomous Build Brief \'97 Location-Aware Plant & Wildlife Identifier PWA\
\
## Mission\
\
Build a polished, mobile-first browser application that lets a user:\
\
1. Open the app on a phone.\
2. Grant camera and location permission.\
3. Take a photo of a plant, flower, animal, insect, fungus, or other organism.\
4. Draw or adjust a bounding box around the organism or feature to identify.\
5. Submit the cropped image.\
6. Receive a ranked identification with:\
   - common name\
   - scientific name\
   - confidence\
   - family/genus where available\
   - useful facts\
   - geographic plausibility\
   - similar/alternative candidates\
   - source attribution\
7. See a clearly separate **\'93From iNaturalist\'94** information box containing nearby observation information.\
8. When confidence is low, show multiple likely candidates and suggest what additional photo would most help.\
9. Install the website to the phone home screen as a PWA.\
10. Use the app over normal cellular data or Wi-Fi from anywhere with internet access.\
\
The first release should focus primarily on **plant identification**, but the architecture, naming, data models, and provider interfaces must be designed from the beginning so that animals, insects, fungi, birds, and other organisms can be added later without rebuilding the application.\
\
The experience should feel like an automated field guide rather than a generic AI chatbot.\
\
---\
\
# CRITICAL AUTONOMY INSTRUCTIONS\
\
You are Claude Code acting as the lead engineer for this project.\
\
## Do not stop to ask the user routine questions.\
\
Make reasonable decisions yourself.\
\
Do not ask about:\
- framework choice\
- database choice\
- UI colors\
- component library\
- naming conventions\
- directory structure\
- API architecture\
- whether to add tests\
- whether to add a PWA\
- whether to handle permissions\
- whether to support iPhone/Android\
- whether to use TypeScript\
- whether to add caching\
- whether to add a results history\
- minor product decisions\
\
Use the defaults in this document.\
\
If a dependency proves unavailable, deprecated, incompatible, or no longer free enough for this prototype, select the closest practical substitute and document the change in `DECISIONS.md`.\
\
Do not block development waiting for an API key.\
\
Implement:\
- the integration\
- environment-variable wiring\
- mocks\
- fallback states\
- developer documentation\
\
so the rest of the application can be completed and tested without the live key.\
\
## The first user question should come only after the application is substantially complete and tested.\
\
At that point ask:\
\
> The app is built and ready to publish. What GitHub repository should I use? Send me the repository URL, `owner/repo`, or tell me to create a new repo and what you want it called.\
\
After receiving the repo location:\
\
- initialize/configure git if necessary\
- commit the finished project\
- push it to the requested repository\
- configure the deployment workflow\
- deploy a publicly reachable HTTPS demo if deployment credentials are already available\
- if an unavoidable external login/API secret is missing, ask only for that specific item\
\
Never put a secret in source control.\
\
---\
\
# Product Working Name\
\
Use the internal working name:\
\
**FieldLens**\
\
This is not intended as permanent branding.\
\
Keep the visible product name centralized in one config/constants file so it can be renamed in seconds.\
\
The application architecture must not contain plant-specific naming at its core.\
\
